EXTENSIONS={
1:('The missing sensor exercise','In the simulated inspection cell, one of two signals is silently lost. Define a watchdog that detects absence of an expected pulse, while explicitly stating what it cannot infer about the real plant. Model three cases: normal arrivals, absent arrivals because the process stopped, and an unplugged sensor. A missing event alone cannot distinguish these cases. Add an independent heartbeat or second signal to improve the diagnostic.','Submit a three-state detection table, a timestamped artificial event sequence, a description of the ambiguity, and a justified operator alert. Do not call the result an emergency interlock.'),
2:('Octal, hexadecimal, and machine words','Select ten 16-bit patterns that include zero, 1, 7, 8, 255, 32767, 32768, and 65535. Display each in octal, hex, binary, and signed decimal. Trace how a conditional branch changes if a value is interpreted as signed rather than unsigned. Do not assume the programmer-visible representation is stored as ASCII characters.','Deliver a representation table and a two-paragraph explanation of what actually changes when the same bits are viewed in another base.'),
3:('Two deadlines and one processor','Create a scheduling simulation with an event every 5 ms requiring 600 µs of service and a reporting task every 100 ms requiring 8 ms of work. Assign priority explicitly and determine whether the proposed service arrangement could miss a deadline when an event arrives at the worst possible instant. Explore an alternative that defers report formatting.','Submit a timeline for at least 100 ms, including event arrival, service start, service completion, and greatest observed response time in the simulation.'),
4:('Measure instruction size rather than judging source size','Prepare MSP430 assembly fragments for a register-register operation, an immediate constant, a far larger immediate, and an indexed memory access. Use the actual assembler listing to count stored 16-bit words. Add a discussion of why the number of textual characters in the source does not measure program memory consumption.','Attach the exact assembler version, code listing, 16-bit opcodes, extension words, and a grounded explanation of any deviations from your prediction.'),
5:('Gate length and apparent precision','Repeat frequency counting at nominal 100 ms, 1000 ms, and 10,000 ms gates, where supported by the actual instrument. Compute edge count, elapsed time, measured frequency, and change between trials. Identify the quantization consequence of counting integral rising edges and how a longer observation can improve resolution without guaranteeing absolute accuracy.','Provide a table of real readings with the actual instrument status and a statement separating frequency resolution, precision, and timebase accuracy.'),
6:('Debounce an unreliable contact','Use an external input record or a simulated bounce sequence to compare two debounce rules: fixed quiet interval and consecutive matching samples. Specify the sampling period and acceptance count before observing the result. Ensure that either rule has a bounded response delay. Identify the tradeoff between latency and false triggers.','Publish a synthetic or measured timestamped edge series, the output of both algorithms, and a calculation of worst-case debounce delay.'),
7:('A histogram of interrupt latency','Use an electrically observable stimulus, a controlled ISR output action, and a timing instrument capable of measuring their separation. Record 100 response intervals if equipment permits. Compute minimum, median, maximum, and 99th-percentile estimate with the sample size clearly stated. Compare this with a hardware timer compare output, ensuring the two tests use appropriate definitions of response.','Publish the raw interval record and a bounded statement about the largest latency observed, without claiming proof of an absolute worst case.'),
8:('Find an intentionally damaged serial record','Build a host-side parser for a sequence-numbered telemetry line. Feed it correctly framed input, a missing field, an invalid unit, a repeated sequence number, and a malformed character. Define whether a bad record is rejected, flagged, or recovered and show that a dropped UART message does not alter the local controller state.','Submit parser tests, an example accepted record, four rejected records, and a short interface contract.'),
9:('Pull-up and rising-edge experiment','Create a controlled RC approximation using a resistor and small capacitor at safe voltage. Measure the rising-edge duration using a scope or a validated high-resolution logic acquisition, then compare two pull-up resistances. Treat the RC model as an approximation; real sensors, wiring, and thresholds may differ. Do not use this substitute as certification of the genuine DS18B20 transaction.','Provide resistor values, observed edge times, the threshold definition used, and a discussion of electrical loading and measurement bandwidth.'),
10:('Thermal lag matters even with correct bytes','Immerse a verified sensor package at a safe, modest temperature step while keeping a second thermometer nearby. Log the time at which the physical environment changes and subsequent sensor readings. Compare two placements such as in air versus shallow water, controlling other conditions. Do not make unsupported claims about the water’s uniform temperature or the sensor’s absolute calibration.','Publish raw timestamped temperature data, CRC validity, a simple response-time estimate, and a note on how geometry changes the measured process.'),
11:('Inject failures before adding a real actuator','Run a platform-independent supervisor with injected CRC faults, timeout faults, readings exactly at 28 and 30 °C, and a rapidly oscillating series near each threshold. Confirm that the state-machine policy is explicit and stable. Do not interpret an LED as proof that a motor driver or fan would be electrically safe.','Deliver a transition matrix with every input, previous state, new state, output request, and reason, plus a separate statement defining the required physical fault output.'),
12:('Reproduce somebody else’s result','Exchange a carefully labeled evidence package with another learner. The receiver must reconstruct the claimed frequency or temperature result without verbal assistance. The receiver should record any missing firmware, wiring, clock settings, sensor identity, or raw data. Revise the package once and show whether the second attempt succeeds.','Deliver both the original and revised evidence-package manifest, the reviewer’s findings, and a specific statement of reproducibility limits.')}
